  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003381",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for the uterine cancer target include uterine/uterus/corpus uteri and endometrial-cancer endpoints. Domain knowledge indicates endometrial carcinoma is the clinically dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma (~90%), so endometrial-cancer PRSs are acceptable direct matches when better supported. Among available candidates, PGS003381 targets uterine endometrial carcinoma with a directly aligned phenotyping_reported endpoint, uses a genome-wide method (lassosum) with high variant count (529,365), and reports a relatively strong full-model AUROC (0.761) in a large European evaluation (n=144,479) with basic covariates (age, PCs). Competing exact-label uterine cancer models (PGS000541/PGS000542) are sparse (18–20 variants) and show notably lower AUROC (~0.57), and the framework-style global biobank score PGS001795 (PRS-CS-auto, 911,692 variants) has weaker reported PRS-only R2 (0.001948) and lower AUROC (0.66) with a framework-study context penalty per domain guidance. Main limitation: PGS003381 reports only full-model metrics (no PRS-only AUROC/incremental AUROC), so the PRS contribution versus covariates cannot be cleanly isolated from the visible metadata, keeping confidence at Moderate."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000342",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches to psoriatic arthritis by EFO, but they differ strongly in study archetype. PGS000342 is disease-focused (publication explicitly evaluates a genetic risk score for diagnosis of psoriatic arthritis) with coherent endpoint labeling (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported both 'Psoriatic arthritis'), which domain guidance prioritizes over framework-derived scores. By contrast, PGS001287 comes from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework ('across 813 traits'), which the provided domain document flags as commonly underperforming in external generalization despite complete metrics. However, the available evidence is limited: PGS000342 reports only a full-model AUROC (0.562) with unknown covariates and no PRS-only metrics, while PGS001287 reports stronger discrimination (full-model AUROC 0.728; PGS-only AUROC 0.676) with basic covariates. This creates unresolved conflict between endpoint-focused study context (favoring PGS000342) and substantially better reported metrics (favoring PGS001287), so the selection remains low-confidence and overall direct-match quality is sub-optimal."
}
